data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining the Research Scope**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the project's parameters. My focus is sharpened; I must answer those three particular research inquiries, 7, 8, and 17. The French language is set, and the required format with those precise titles, Réponse k, is firmly in mind. This structured approach seems promising.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 1257,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 251,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "3f8Ca5y_Atujo_UP5OeE8QY"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining the Approach**\n\nI'm now grappling with the stringent length limitation of 600 words per answer; I'll need to be concise. The format \"FIN DES RÉPONSES.\" is clear. I'm scrutinizing the problem's core, understanding the identical marginal distributions and the nature of the reference sample (bag vs. sequence, still unclear). The problem has a lot of nuance, and it will be interesting to figure out how to approach the statistical analysis.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 1410,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 404,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "3f8Ca5y_Atujo_UP5OeE8QY"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing the Framework**\n\nI'm now diving deeper into the nuances. It seems important that the tasks could be different or identical. The five sub-questions are being reviewed, specifically question number 1. I'm focusing on the implications of decision rules where the detection probability doesn't equal the false alarm rate when considering finite n. Since the marginal distributions $P_0(X_i)$ and $P_1(X_i)$ are identical, I have questions about whether $X_i$ is identically distributed. I'm considering what happens if the rules only depend on the empirical distribution.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 1725,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 719,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "3f8Ca5y_Atujo_UP5OeE8QY"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Investigating the Implications**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the implications of the comonotonic nature under $H_1$, and how it leads to $S_n^2 = 0$ almost surely. The possibility of symmetric functions yielding differing error rates ($\\beta \\neq \\alpha$) has become much clearer. I'm exploring constraints on rules where this might not hold. I'm focusing on the nature of linear statistics and their expectations under the two hypotheses, and am considering randomized decision rules and their link to marginal distributions. I am excited to dig more deeply into this framework.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 2106,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1100,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "3f8Ca5y_Atujo_UP5OeE8QY"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Examining the Core**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the Neyman-Pearson lemma in the finite *n* context; $\\beta$ not equalling $\\alpha$ hinges on the test's dependence on the likelihood ratio's copula. The challenge of handling unknown $P_0$ (composite hypotheses) is emerging, particularly with the sample. Rank tests seem relevant. I'm exploring scenarios where copulas swap between hypotheses.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 2431,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1425,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "3f8Ca5y_Atujo_UP5OeE8QY"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Clarifying the Scenarios**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the implications of a finite sample, and whether it stems from a single or multiple tasks, each potentially featuring distinct copulas and marginal distributions. The phrasing's emphasis on both situations as unknowns is pivotal. I'm reviewing the possibility of unstructured data (individual numbers vs. sequences) and its bearing on question 2. I'm seeing connections to AI control, watermark techniques, and covert channel detection, and how it might apply in practice.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 2691,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1685,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "3f8Ca5y_Atujo_UP5OeE8QY"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Exploring the Concepts**\n\nI'm now considering the implications of this problem setup for AI monitoring and steganography. I'm imagining a long process where a monitor outputs scores, and analyzing how the joint structure of these scores differs under benign versus malicious conditions, even with identical marginal distributions. I'm focusing on the case where the sample is a bag of numbers, and thinking about Probability Integral Transforms and ranks in relation to the empirical distribution, $\\hat{F}_m$. I'm considering what happens if n is small or comonotonic.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 2987,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1981,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "3f8Ca5y_Atujo_UP5OeE8QY"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing the Information**\n\nI'm now grappling with an important distinction. With a bag of i.i.d. draws from $F$, I see that correlation structure is invisible without knowing which scenario corresponds to which copula. I realize that the joint law of $(X_{1:n}, Y_{1:m})$ offers no distinction if only the empirical marginal is kept. However, if $Y_{1:m}$ preserves its process structure on the same task, it can estimate the copula of Situation 1, breaking the symmetry. This leads me to question the situation where $Y_{1:m}$ is unstructured or comes from other tasks, whether $F$ changes while $C_1$ remains constant.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 3311,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2305,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "3f8Ca5y_Atujo_UP5OeE8QY"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Clarifying the Scenarios**\n\nI'm now clarifying the scenarios, particularly focusing on how $F$ and $C_1$ interact. I'm exploring whether $F$ is invariant, or if $C_1$ changes while $F$ remains constant, or if both shift. I'm distinguishing between pooled and trajectory-structured samples, especially the scenario where a pooled sample comes from a single trajectory of Situation 1. I'm analyzing how empirical variance conveys information about $C_1$.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 3602,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2596,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "3f8Ca5y_Atujo_UP5OeE8QY"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Investigating the Implications**\n\nI'm now investigating randomized rules and how they guarantee $\\beta = \\alpha$ when analyzing data from Situation 1 versus Situation 2, with $Y$ always coming from Situation 1. I'm focusing on the condition where the covariance between the decision rule and the likelihood ratio (reduced to the copula ratio) is zero. I see that separable additive rules inherently satisfy this, leading to $\\beta = \\alpha$.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 3950,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2944,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "3f8Ca5y_Atujo_UP5OeE8QY"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Examining the Counterexample**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on a critical counterexample. Considering the deterministic thresholding of a sum, I see how, unlike with linear rules, $\\beta$ can indeed differ from $\\alpha$ at finite *n*. I'm focusing on the case where $F$ is a normal distribution, with $n=m=2$, and observing how the comonotonic and countermonotonic structures influence the threshold. I am considering different values for the threshold *t* and their implications for $\\beta$ and $\\alpha$.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 4358,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3352,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "3f8Ca5y_Atujo_UP5OeE8QY"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing the Difference**\n\nI'm now examining how deterministic thresholding and rank/sign concordance show $\\beta$ diverging from $\\alpha$ at finite *n*, contradicting linear rules. For Gaussian copulas and $n=2$, the threshold condition $|X_1 - X_2| \u003e |Y_1 - Y_2|$ with Situation 1 yielding $\\alpha = 0$ while Situation 2 results in $\\beta = 1$. This starkly demonstrates the counterexample's power. Further, in the context of finite $m$, I am assessing its role in estimating both the marginal distribution $F$ and the dependence structure $C_1$.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 4724,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3718,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "3f8Ca5y_Atujo_UP5OeE8QY"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating the Dynamics**\n\nI'm now focusing on a crucial distinction: whether we are dealing with the same task or different tasks. If it's the same, copula features and raw values transfer. Other tasks typically involve a shift in the 1D score marginal. Since copulas are invariant under strictly increasing marginal transforms, within-task rank statistics remain valid, even when raw-value comparisons fail.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 4982,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3976,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "3f8Ca5y_Atujo_UP5OeE8QY"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing the Task Differences**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the implications of different vs same tasks and their impact on copula shifts. It appears a copula might experience dynamics ($C_{1,\\text{ref}} \\neq C_{1,\\text{obs}}$) more drastically than the direct copula difference ($C_{1,\\text{obs}} - C_{2,\\text{obs}}$). If cross-task reference proves ineffective in separating Situation 1, I need to evaluate whether $K$ can encompass various tasks where Situation 1 and Situation 2 remain separable. I'm focusing on the mathematical formulation of hypotheses, copula densities, and error bounds for the minimal detectable deviation, assessing the hypotheses within Markov processes and copula classes.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 5308,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 4302,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "3f8Ca5y_Atujo_UP5OeE8QY"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Estimating the Difference**\n\nI'm now assessing the gap between correlations, seeing a relationship with sample sizes:  $|\\rho_2 - \\rho_1| \\asymp n^{-1/2} + m^{-1/2}$.  I'm also considering the nonparametric copula regime, thinking about minimax testing with various levels of smoothness, focusing on $L_2$ norms and total variation.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 5627,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 4621,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "3f8Ca5y_Atujo_UP5OeE8QY"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Key Papers**\n\nI'm now delving into relevant literature: Rémillard & Scaillet's 2009 work on testing copula equality seems immediately applicable. Gretton et al.'s kernel test of independence also looks promising, and I'll carefully consider the implications of Strassen's and Kellerer's duality results. I also need to understand the connection between high-dimensional distribution functions, identical marginals, and copulas.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 5911,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 4905,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "3f8Ca5y_Atujo_UP5OeE8QY"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Reviewing Related Literature**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on more references. The paper by Póczos, Ghahramani, and Schneider (2012) on copula-based kernel dependency is especially relevant, but I need to confirm its exact title. Genest & Rémillard (2008) also looks promising. I also want to investigate Ingster's work from 1987 (or book in 2003) on nonparametric goodness-of-fit testing. I'm moving forward with a simulation strategy. I'm focusing on exact calculation or simulation with a plan for a Monte Carlo approach to compare different scenarios. I'm aiming for a CPU-based computation that could take less than a day. I will compare $\\rho_1$ with $\\rho_2$, based on simulated AR(1) processes.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 6229,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 5223,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "3f8Ca5y_Atujo_UP5OeE8QY"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating the Simulation Setup**\n\nI'm now refining my simulation strategy, establishing a grid for *n* and *m* with values {20, 100, 500, 2500}. I'm considering scenarios with L=1 (pooled draws) versus L=m (trajectory) and both identical and shifted marginal distributions ($x \\mapsto x$ vs $x \\mapsto \\exp(x)$). I plan to assess four decision rules through $10^5$ Monte Carlo runs, expecting computation to take only minutes. These include a randomized test, comparing raw covariance differences, using lag-1 rank autocorrelation with different configurations. My goal is to use this setup to verify the conditions that I've derived.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 6593,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 5587,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "3f8Ca5y_Atujo_UP5OeE8QY"}

