data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining the Refutation Approach**\n\nI'm now clarifying the falsification strategy for this assessment. I'm focusing on two key approaches: a **bibliographic search** and a **concrete falsifying experiment**. For the bibliographic search, I'll use targeted queries on Semantic Scholar/arXiv to find papers about LLM agents, AI control, and related concepts. The experiment will either demonstrate that LLMs easily detect traps or infer horizon position. Alternatively, it will show that simple deterministic techniques achieve the best performance.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 8701,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 7695,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "3f8Ca5y_Atujo_UP5OeE8QY"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining the Refutation**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on a powerful falsification approach. The key lies in demonstrating how simple deterministic approaches can outperform, thereby rendering complex techniques unnecessary. This could involve either proving LLMs can readily identify traps or showing that they can infer horizon positions, undermining the efficacy of randomized challenges. This two-pronged approach targets both under-researched areas (bibliographic) and potentially promising methods (experimental) to strengthen the refutation.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 9006,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 8000,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "3f8Ca5y_Atujo_UP5OeE8QY"}

